6. What is the default initial capacity of an ArrayList?
 default capacity is 10 but we can provide also the initial capacity with the method new ArrayList<>(int capacity);
7. How does ArrayList grow when it exceeds capacity? What's the growth factor (1.5x in Java)?
as soon as it exceeds capacity arraylist internally creates new array of size 1.5 times to its and copies
old array element into new array via the help of method System.arrayCopy();
8. What is the time complexity of add(), get(), remove(), contains() in ArrayList?
time complexity differs for add() remove() but for get() or contains its remains constant b/c of
contigious memory allocation
9. Why is add(index, element) and remove(index) costly in ArrayList (O(n)) compared to LinkedList?
  while adding  and removing element at  specific index all elements needs to get shift from that position to
  next +1 or -1 location
10. What happens internally when you call ensureCapacity() or trimToSize()?
What happens internally when you call ensureCapacity() or trimToSize()?

ensureCapacity(int minCapacity): Pre-emptively resizes the internal
array to at least the specified capacity before you start adding elements.
This is useful when you know in advance that you're going to insert a large number of
elements — it avoids multiple incremental O(n) resize/copy operations by doing one upfront
allocation instead.
trimToSize(): Shrinks the internal array's capacity down to exactly match
the current number of elements (size), discarding any unused reserved slots.
This is useful for freeing up memory after a list has had many elements removed and
is left holding onto excess allocated capacity.
11. Is ArrayList thread-safe? How do you make it thread-safe?
