questions


Medium
Sort an ArrayList of custom objects (e.g., Employee) by multiple fields using Comparator chaining (thenComparing).
Demonstrate the classic remove(int index) vs remove(Object) trap using an ArrayList<Integer> — show how list.remove(1) behaves differently depending on whether 1 is passed as a primitive int vs a boxed Integer.
Safely remove elements from an ArrayList while iterating (using Iterator.remove() or removeIf()), and show what goes wrong if you try list.remove() directly inside a for-each loop.
Demonstrate ConcurrentModificationException with a for-each loop on an ArrayList, then fix it.
Flatten a nested ArrayList (List<List<Integer>>) into a single ArrayList.
Find all pairs in an ArrayList that sum to a target value.
Partition an ArrayList into sublists of a given size (chunking).
Implement your own simple dynamic array (mini-ArrayList) from scratch, including manual resizing logic (simulate the 1.5x growth + System.arraycopy behavior).
Given an ArrayList, group consecutive duplicate elements together (run-length encoding).
Demonstrate how subList() on an ArrayList is a view, not a copy — show a modification to the sublist reflecting on the original list, then show how to avoid it (new ArrayList<>(list.subList(...))).
Write a program to demonstrate ensureCapacity() and trimToSize() — use reflection or timing to show reduced resize operations when ensureCapacity() is called upfront.
Hard / Design-Oriented
Design an LRU cache using ArrayList + HashMap combo (without LinkedHashMap).
Compare and benchmark performance of ArrayList vs LinkedList for: insert-at-front, insert-at-end, insert-at-middle, and random access on a large dataset (e.g., 100,000 elements) — print timing results and explain why.
Implement a thread-safe wrapper around ArrayList using synchronized blocks manually, then compare its behavior/performance to CopyOnWriteArrayList under a high-read, low-write multithreaded scenario.
Design a data structure using ArrayList that supports O(1) insert, O(1) delete (of an arbitrary element, not by index), and O(1) getRandom() — this is the classic "Insert Delete GetRandom O(1)" problem, typically solved with ArrayList + HashMap.
Given a stream of integers arriving one at a time, maintain and return the median efficiently — while not strictly ArrayList-only, a naive/ArrayList-based approach (insert into sorted position) is a common follow-up before introducing heaps.